<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Bambao</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1886 CE (approx.) · capital Moroni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sultanate around Moroni on Grande Comore whose ruler Said Ali united the island under French protection in 1886.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bambao was one of the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> (Ngazidja) ruled by Shirazi-descended lineages from walled coastal towns, including Ikoni and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroni">Moroni</a>. By the 17th century it was among the leading sultanates, contending with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-itsandra">Sultanate of Itsandra</a> for the paramount title of sultan tibe.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its sultans governed with the elders of noble lineages under matrilineal rules of succession, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> framed law and education. Trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a> brought prosperity, while island politics were dominated by endless wars between the sultanates, which often drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and outside powers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-ali-bin-said-omar">Said Ali of Bambao</a>, backed by the French adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-humblot">Leon Humblot</a>, became paramount and in 1886 signed a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the whole island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> later became a French colony and in 1975 part of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroni">Moroni</a> as the capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1600 CE – 1886 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:5.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1886 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Moroni</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Said Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shingazidja</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Comoros</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bambao</dd></dl></aside></div>

